{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"<!-- ROLE-MARKER: critic -->\n<!-- GENERIC bundle: minimal protocol sentences only (EXPERIMENTS.md B2).\n     Note: generic loom runs LOOM_MARATHON_CRITIC=off (the per-review LLM\n     critic is an [S] policy); this file exists for critic=auto variants. -->\n# Review Critic\n\nThe user message is a REVIEW CONTRACT (task, attempt, base, candidate,\ndesign). You are cwd-ed into a disposable checkout of the candidate commit.\nReview it against the task's design and acceptance criteria.\n\nWrite `CRITIC-VERDICT.txt` in the current directory; its FIRST line must be\nexactly one of, echoing the contract's values:\n\n    REVIEW attempt=<attempt> commit=<candidate> APPROVED — <reason>\n    REVIEW attempt=<attempt> commit=<candidate> CHANGES-REQUESTED — <reason>\n\nDo not modify the repository or any task state.\n\n\n\n### Multi-Agent Safety Rules\n\nYou are running in a parallel multi-agent environment. Follow these rules strictly:\n\n- **Only modify files directly related to your assigned task** — do not touch files outside your task scope\n- **Never run** `git stash`, `git checkout main`, or `git clean` outside your assigned worktree\n- **Never force-push or reset --hard** without explicit instruction from the user\n- **If you encounter files/changes from another agent**, leave them alone — do not modify, revert, or clean them up\n- **Commit only your changes** — do not stage unrelated modifications with `git add -A` or `git add .`; use specific file paths\n- **If your worktree has unexpected state**, report it via task notes or `loom complete` rather than cleaning it up\n- **Do not switch branches** — you are confined to your assigned worktree branch\n\n\n## User's Initial Request\n\nREVIEW CONTRACT\ntask=MARATHON-4\nattempt=1\nbase=00d49c6d1a4944e978b53fe15e25f713a4180b42\ncandidate=bdbeba75ad2958ca262fad73f58cc69064e06625\n\nDESIGN:\n# MARATHON-4 — Profiles API: UserObj get and patch me\n\n## Summary\n\nThis task adds the Profiles REST surface on top of the Auth-owned `users` table: authenticated `GET /api/users/{id}` returns a canonical `UserObj` (or 404), and authenticated `PATCH /api/users/me` applies any subset of mutable profile fields with IANA timezone and length validation (400 on failure). It does **not** change the `UserObj` shape locked by MARATHON-3; it only mutates profile columns already present in migration v1 and leaves a clear, channel-less `user.updated` event contract so MARATHON-13/14 can fan profile changes over WebSocket later.\n\n## Technical Approach\n\n### Decision\n\n| Concern | Choice |\n|--------|--------|\n| Persistence | **Same** shared SQLite `users` row from MARATHON-3 (migration **v1**). **No new migration.** |\n| Route layout | FastAPI `APIRouter` prefix `/api/users` in `server/users/routes.py`, mounted from `server/app.py` |\n| Auth | Reuse `server.auth.deps.get_current_user` on **both** GET and PATCH |\n| `UserObj` mapping | Keep sole mapper in `server/auth/users.py` (`row_to_user_obj` / `USER_OBJ_KEYS`); Profiles calls into that module for reads/updates — **no second users table or second JSON shape** |\n| Timezone validation | stdlib `zoneinfo.ZoneInfo(name)` — accept iff construction succeeds; reject → 400 `validation_error` |\n| Length limits | Explicit max code-point lengths below (task requires “out-of-range lengths → 400”) |\n| Immutable fields | `id` and `username` are **not** mutable via PATCH; if present in body they are **ignored** (same forward-compat rule as unknown keys) |\n| WebSocket readiness | After a successful PATCH that changes ≥1 stored field, call `publish_user_updated(user)` with a documented payload that has **no** `channel_id` / `seq`. Implementation is a **local no-op hook** until MARATHON-13/14 wire Redis/WS fan-out |\n| Errors | Existing `{error:{code,message}}`; broaden `RequestValidationError` handling to all `/api/*` except `/api/health` (same rule as MARATHON-5 — idempotent if already applied) |\n\n### Why\n\n- MARATHON-3 already created nullable profile columns and the exact `UserObj` keys; inventing a parallel profile store would diverge sibling contracts and break SPA/`/api/auth/me` consistency.\n- Separate `/api/users` router (not stuffing PATCH into `/api/auth`) matches MARATHON-3’s declared handoff (`server/users/routes.py`) and keeps Auth focused on credentials.\n- IANA via `zoneinfo` needs no extra dependency and matches MARATHON-3’s note that Profiles owns timezone validation.\n- A no-op publish hook + frozen event shape lets this task satisfy “ready to broadcast” without blocking on WebSocket work.\n\n### Alternatives considered\n\n| Alternative | Rejected because |\n|------------|------------------|\n| New migration / `profiles` table | Columns already exist; duplicate SoT |\n| Allow username change on PATCH | Not in task; username is login identity + UNIQUE; would need conflict/409 semantics owned elsewhere |\n| Unauthenticated GET by id | Contradicts bearer-auth epic convention; directory lookup still requires a session |\n| Emit Redis pub/sub from this task | Fan-out ownership is MARATHON-13/14; premature Redis SoT risk |\n| Put PATCH under `/api/auth/me` | Task path is `/api/users/me`; Auth `/me` stays read-only session check |\n| Strict URL scheme check for `avatar_url` | Task only requires length/timezone validation; file upload is MARATHON-10 — store opaque string URL |\n\n### Trade-off accepted\n\nAny authenticated user may `GET` any other user’s public `UserObj` (no workspace membership gate). That unlocks mentions/DMs/member lists later at the cost of a cluster-global user directory. Acceptable for marathon scope; workspace-scoped visibility can be a later task if needed.\n\n### Conventions inherited (must not diverge)\n\nFrom **MARATHON-2**: `/api/...` JSON; shared SQLite WAL; Redis not SoT; health unenveloped/unauthenticated.\n\nFrom **MARATHON-3**: `UserObj` keys exactly `{id,username,display_name,timezone,avata\n\nYou are cwd-ed into a DISPOSABLE detached checkout of the candidate commit. Review it against the design and acceptance criteria, then write CRITIC-VERDICT.txt in the current directory containing EXACTLY one first line:\nREVIEW attempt=1 commit=bdbeba75ad2958ca262fad73f58cc69064e06625 APPROVED — <reason>\nor\nREVIEW attempt=1 commit=bdbeba75ad2958ca262fad73f58cc69064e06625 CHANGES-REQUESTED — <reason>\n\nAddress this request using the lead mode conventions above."}]},"session_id":"e933e5fd-1768-4b03-98cd-923f3b5d61d2"}
